e><surname>Sachdev</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A monotonie digitally controlled delay element</article-title><source><italic>IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits</italic></source><year>2005</year><volume>40</volume><issue>11</issue><lpage>2212</lpage><lpage>2219</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><label>30</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rieger</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Demosthenous</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Taylor</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A 230-nW 10-s time constant CMOS integrator for an adaptive nerve signal amplifier</article-title><source><italic>IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits</italic></source><year>2004</year><volume>39</volume><issue>11</issue><lpage>1968</lpage><lpage>1975</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Wearable wireless interface for the heart monitoring: system idea.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Prototype of the inner garment in which the wearable wireless interface for the detection of the heart and breath rates will be included. In detail, the sensor will be placed around the circled area.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Future perspective: integration on a single silicon chip of the overall wearable wireless interface on a single silicon chip.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Block diagram of the proposed fully integrated UWB radar for the detection of heart and breath rates.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Channel loss versus frequency predicted by the near-field-based model.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Power spectral density (PSD) of a pulse sequence with PRF equal to 1&#x02009;MHz versus frequency.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Gaussian monocycle pulse and pulse at the output of the antenna.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Voltage signal at the input of the LNA, including the noise contributions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.008"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Voltage at the output of the integrator. The output signal has the same frequency of the movement imposed for the heart wall. A time-varying surface with a period of 20 milliseconds has been considered for the simulation (this period is short with respect to the real heart moving, in order to reduce the simulation time. This does not impair the analysis since the radar reaches widely the steady state within ten milliseconds).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.009"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Antenna prototype for the channel model verification.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.010"/></fig><fig id="fig11" position="float"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>Setup for the channel-loss measurement, setup between the front and the back of a human chest.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.011"/></fig><fig id="fig12" position="float"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Measurement results of the intrabody channel loss.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.012"/></fig><fig id="fig13" position="float"><label>Figure 13</label><caption><p>Prototype of UWB antenna realized.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.013"/></fig><fig id="fig14" position="float"><label>Figure 14</label><caption><p>Simulated (blue) and measured (red) S11 parameters of the UWB antenna of <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig13">Figure 13</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJTA2008-328597.014"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>